$DIR/main.p:14:1: onPanic is the panic handler, so it must never return
    A panic stops the program: it does not unwind, so there is nothing for the
    handler to return to. Every path through it must end in an endless loop, or
    in a call of a function that never returns, such as os.Exit, or a function
    whose every path ends the same way. A call through a func value or an
    interface, and recursion, count as returning.
    onPanic can return here (main.p:14).
    A kernel has no operating system to fall back on when it panics, so
    Plutonium makes the program choose what happens at compile time, and checks
    the choice: the runtime calls the handler directly, with nothing to
    register, so it works from the first instruction.
    End it by halting:
        for {
            x86.Cli()
            x86.Hlt()
        }
    (see "Panics in freestanding programs" in the Plutonium spec, section 17.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2583" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:31:2: keyboard is an interrupt handler, so it must not wait
    Interrupt handlers run on top of whatever code the CPU was executing. If a
    handler waits, for a lock or for room in a channel, the interrupted code can
    never resume to release it, and the CPU stops.
    keyboard calls readConfig (main.p:31), which calls cfgLock.Lock (main.p:27),
    which may be (*Mutex).Lock, which may wait.
    cfgLock.Lock calls through an interface, so it counts as calling the Lock
    method of every type the program converts to main.Locker.
    A kernel that waits in an interrupt handler hangs, often only under load and
    far from the cause, so Plutonium checks the whole program at compile time
    instead.
    To hand work to normal code, use a non-blocking send:
        select { case work <- item: default: }
    (see "Interrupts" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.10)
    (run "pluto explain E2584" for a longer explanation)
